[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163206 :tutorial/content "Suppose a bag contains $10$ candies, $4$ of which are blue and $6$ are orange. We randomly select $3$ candies from the bag *without replacement* and count the number of blue candies.\n\nLet's assume we run this experiment and draw the following sequence:\n$\\stackrel{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}{\\textrm{blue}},\\qquad {\\color{black}{\\textrm{orange}}},\\qquad \\stackrel{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}{\\textrm{blue}}$\n\nA diagram like the one shown below can help us visualize this process. It helps to imagine the bag containing two distinct sets of candies (blue and orange).\n\n![](images/c3/c3d4a81213cdbabeacca7e392ca2e2234df3f10c5682c7de8f5edeaa05de03cf.png)\n\nLet's interpret choosing a blue candy as a \"success\" and orange as a \"failure.\"\n\nLet the random variable $X$ equal the number of successes (blue candies in the sample). In the example above, we have $X=2$. The probability\n\n$$\nP(X=2)\n$$\n\nis given by the following ratio:\n\n$$\nP(X = 2) \n= \\dfrac{ {\\text{number of samples of size } {3} \\text{ with } {2} \\text{ blue candies}}}{ {\\text{number of samples of size } {3}}}\n$$\n\nThe denominator represents all possible ways to choose $3$ candies from $10$, while the numerator represents only those cases where exactly $2$ are blue.\n\nTo calculate this probability, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1**: Determine the number of samples of size $3$ containing $2$ blue candies:The number of ways of selecting $2$ blue candies from a total of $4$ in the population is given by\n$\\displaystyle {}_{4}C_{2} = \\binom{4}{2}$.\nThe number of ways of selecting $1$ orange candy from a total of $6$ in the population is\n$\\displaystyle {}_{6}C_{1} = \\binom{6}{1}$.\nSo, by the *rule of product,* the number of samples of size $3$ with precisely $2$ blue candies is\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{# of ways of selecting } {2} \\text{ blue candies} \\hspace{0.222em} \\times  \\hspace{0.222em} \\text{# of ways of selecting } {1} \\text{ orange candy} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2} \\right) \\cdot  \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{1} \\right) .\\end{array}}\n$$.\n(Note. The symbol $\\textrm{#}$ is shorthand for \"number of\").\n- **Step 2**: Determine the total number of samples of size $3$.This is simply the number of ways of selecting $3$ objects from a population of $10$. This is given by\n${}_{10}C_{3} = \\binom{10}{3}$.\n- **Step 3**: Divide the results.Therefore, we conclude that\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=2) &= \\frac{\\substack{\\text{\\# of samples of size 3}\\\\\\text{with precisely 2 blue candies}}}{\\text{\\# of samples of size 3}} \\\\ &= \\frac{\\binom{4}{2}\\cdot\\binom{6}{1}}{\\binom{10}{3}} \\\\ &= \\frac{3}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n.\n\nThis same reasoning applies in more general situations where we sample without replacement. If we have a population of $N$ objects, where $K$ are \"successes,\" and we randomly draw $n$ objects, then the number of successes follows a so-called **hypergeometric distribution,** and we write\n\n$$\nX \\sim \\textrm{Hypergeometric}(N, K, n)\n$$\n\nIn our example, we had\n$X\\sim \\textrm{Hypergeometric}(10, 4, 3)$,\nsince there were $N=10$ candies, $K=4$ blue candies (successes), and we were considering samples of size $n=3$." :tutorial/id #uuid "2d85a9b5-b913-59b9-8314-47ad73ebfded" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10876 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205176 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we will learn how the hypergeometric distribution can be used to model real-world situations. We'll also discuss the structure of a hypergeometric random variable's probability mass function (PMF).\n\nSuppose a chess club has $18$ members, of whom $12$ are veteran players and $6$ are beginners. A sample of $6$ players is randomly chosen to participate in a chess tournament. If $X$ represents the number of veterans in the sample, find an expression that represents\n\n$$\nP(X = x)\n$$\n\nfor $X \\in \\{0,1,2,3,4,5,6\\}$.\n\nThe probability that the sample contains precisely $x$ veteran players is given by\n$$\nP(X = x) = \\dfrac{ {\\text{# of samples of size } {6} \\text{ with } {x} \\text{ successes}}}{ {\\text{# of samples of size } {6} \\text{ }}}.\n$$\n\nHere, a \"success\" means selecting a veteran player, and a \"failure\" means choosing a beginner.\n\nLet's calculate the numerator and denominator separately.\n\n- First, let's find the number of samples of size $6$ with precisely $x$ veteran players. The number of ways of selecting $x$ veteran players from a total of $12$ in the population is\n$\\displaystyle {}_{12}C_{x} = \\binom{12}{x}$.\nThe other $6-x$ players in the sample are beginners. The number of ways of selecting $6-x$ beginners from a total of $18-12=6$ in the population is\n$\\displaystyle {}_{6}C_{6-x} = \\binom{6}{6-x}$.\nSo, by the rule of product, the number of samples of size $6$ with precisely $x$ veteran players is\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\text{\\# of samples with }x\\text{ veteran players} \\\\ &= \\left(\\substack{\\text{\\# of ways to select }x\\\\\\text{veteran players}}\\right)\\cdot\\left(\\substack{\\text{\\# of ways to select }(6-x)\\\\\\text{beginners}}\\right) \\\\ &= \\binom{12}{x}\\cdot\\binom{6}{6-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n.\n- Next, we find the number of samples of size $6$ from a population of size $18$. This is given by\n${}_{18}C_{6} = \\binom{18}{6}$.\n\nTherefore, for $X \\in \\{0,1,2,3,4,5,6\\}$, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle P ( X = x ) & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{# of samples of size } {6} \\text{ with } {x} \\text{ successes}}{\\text{# of samples of size } {6}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\displaystyle \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{ {\\color{blue}x}}} \\right) \\cdot  \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{ {\\color{blue}6}}}{\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{ {\\color{blue}6 - x}}} \\right)}}{ {\\displaystyle \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{ {\\color{blue}18}}}{6} \\right)}}} .\\end{array}}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2a7cc148-69d0-50a7-a2a9-1084e9317512" :tutorial/math-academy-id 11563 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]
